### Description

For error messages ("last read: '...'"), the lexer keeps the text of the token it is reading. The token start is only moved by `reset()`, which only strings and numbers call, so after a string or number everything that follows (whitespace, structural characters, literals) still belongs to that "token" until the next string or number starts. For contiguous input this costs nothing, because the text is rebuilt from positions on error. For the other inputs it is copied while parsing:

- `std::istream` and `FILE*`: the adapters keep the token themselves; on every refill of the stream buffer the token's part of the old buffer is appended to a carry buffer ([`input_adapters.hpp`](https://github.com/nlohmann/json/blob/5bad6c518bf6663b69f505511a19c98c91f77c3a/include/nlohmann/detail/input/input_adapters.hpp), `save_token_segment()`).
- input iterators and other adapters that cannot be read again: the lexer copies every character into `token_string` ([`lexer.hpp`](https://github.com/nlohmann/json/blob/5bad6c518bf6663b69f505511a19c98c91f77c3a/include/nlohmann/detail/input/lexer.hpp), `capture_char()`).

So **valid** input with a long run of whitespace after a string or number keeps all of it in memory, and the same holds for the raw text of a long string (kept once more next to the parsed value).

The text is only used for error messages (and the `last_token` of `parse_error()` in SAX), and a message that shows several megabytes of input is not useful anyway.

**Since when:** 3.12.0 behaves the same (225 MB for each of the three inputs with 100,000,000 newlines); there it is the lexer's `token_string`, which copied every character for all of them.
**Security:** Memory amplification on valid input, linear (about 2.25 bytes per input byte, from vector doubling): a sender can make a server that parses JSON from a socket stream or a file hold as much memory as it sends, beyond the parsed value.
**Related:** #6109 (the size of the message itself after a parse error).

### Reproduction steps

1. Save the program below as `mem.cpp`; it parses `1` followed by 100,000,000 newlines from a generating `std::streambuf` (the input is never in memory), from a `FILE*` (`funopen`), or from an input iterator.
2. `clang++ -std=c++17 -O2 -DNDEBUG -I include mem.cpp -o mem`
3. `/usr/bin/time -l ./mem istream 100000000` (and `file`, `iter`), compare with `1000`.

### Expected vs. actual results

- **Expected:** memory independent of the number of newlines, as for `std::string` input.
- **Actual:** peak resident memory grows with the input (Apple clang 21, `-O2`, macOS arm64):

| input | 1,000 newlines | 100,000,000 newlines |
|---|---:|---:|
| `std::istream` | 1.8 MB | 224.8 MB |
| `FILE*` | 1.8 MB | 225.3 MB |
| input iterator | 1.8 MB | 225.3 MB |

### Minimal code example

```cpp
// usage: ./mem <istream|file|iter> <newlines>: parses "1" + newlines without holding the input in memory
#include <nlohmann/json.hpp>
#include <cstdio>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <iostream>
#include <iterator>
#include <streambuf>
#include <string>

static std::size_t total = 0;
static char at(std::size_t i) { return i == 0 ? '1' : '\n'; }

struct gen_buf : std::streambuf
{
    char buf[65536];
    std::size_t pos = 0;
    int_type underflow() override
    {
        if (pos >= total) { return traits_type::eof(); }
        std::size_t n = 0;
        while (n < sizeof(buf) && pos < total) { buf[n++] = at(pos++); }
        setg(buf, buf, buf + n);
        return traits_type::to_int_type(buf[0]);
    }
};

static std::size_t fpos = 0;
static int read_cb(void*, char* b, int n) { int k = 0; while (k < n && fpos < total) { b[k++] = at(fpos++); } return k; }

struct gen_it
{
    using iterator_category = std::input_iterator_tag;
    using value_type = char;
    using difference_type = std::ptrdiff_t;
    using pointer = const char*;
    using reference = char;
    std::size_t i;
    char operator*() const { return at(i); }
    gen_it& operator++() { ++i; return *this; }
    gen_it operator++(int) { auto t = *this; ++i; return t; }
    bool operator==(const gen_it& o) const { return i == o.i; }
    bool operator!=(const gen_it& o) const { return i != o.i; }
};

int main(int argc, char** argv)
{
    const std::string kind = argv[1];
    total = 1 + std::strtoull(argv[2], nullptr, 10);
    nlohmann::json j;
    if (kind == "istream") { gen_buf b; std::istream is(&b); j = nlohmann::json::parse(is); }
    else if (kind == "file") { FILE* f = funopen(nullptr, read_cb, nullptr, nullptr, nullptr); j = nlohmann::json::parse(f); fclose(f); }
    else { j = nlohmann::json::parse(gen_it{0}, gen_it{total}); }
    std::cout << kind << ": " << j.dump() << '\n';
}
```

### Error messages

```
none: the parse succeeds (prints "istream: 1"); see the memory table above
```

### Compiler and operating system

Apple clang 21.0.0 (clang-2100.4.1.1), macOS 27.0.1, arm64

### Library version

develop (5bad6c518b)

